WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:07.440
A lack of water and extreme heat is&nbsp;
holding the Western United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.120
hostage. Wildfires started early this season.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:18.560
Here in Oregon the so-called Bootleg fire has&nbsp;
already destroyed an area bigger than Los Angeles.&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.600
The mega fire is burning so hot and&nbsp;
generating so much energy, that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.360
is essentially creating its own weather,&nbsp;
spreading smoke as far as New York City.&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.600
The fire is moving so fast, that many&nbsp;
ranchers weren’t able to rescue their cattle.&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.600
More than a hundred families have&nbsp;
already lost their homes to the flames&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.000
while thousands were evacuated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:52.480
It’s totally unclear how long it will take&nbsp;
fire fighters to get the flames under control.&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:59.360
Many roads are still closed. The National&nbsp;
Guard has been deployed to control traffic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.920
with only ranchers allowed in to&nbsp;
check on their cattle in many areas.&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:11.120
The little community of Sprague River is&nbsp;
only miles away from the edge of the fire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.360
Summer has been living here for more than 20 years&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.680
There is probably hundreds of cattle that&nbsp;
have been lost, and there is probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.880
hundreds of cattle that are still out there.&nbsp;
The Janes lost a bunch, the Jamsie Ranch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.520
Joe Jane they lost some, the Galgers out here,&nbsp;
the Whiskey Creek Ranch, which are like my family.&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.000
The fire department put out information&nbsp;
signs in the whole area to keep people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.520
updated. Brent and his wife just&nbsp;
moved here to start a life as farmers.&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:53.280
This fire has been much bigger. Seeing the&nbsp;
clouds from it and just seeing the containment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:59.120
line only be about this big right here for&nbsp;
days and days and days. It’s been a big one.&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:05.840
When the wind blows that hard and the fire&nbsp;
is that big, it’s kind of hard to stop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:11.280
especially this dry. But I know that they&nbsp;
are rationing ground water around here too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:18.000
Farmers that are newer to farming,&nbsp;
it’s on a first-come first-serve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.840
as far as how long you had your well.&nbsp;
And how long you have been using it.&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.200
Year to year it is becoming clearer:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:35.840
access to water is defining who will be able&nbsp;
to survive in this area, and who won’t make it.&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:44.480
And it is already a reality, that there&nbsp;
is not enough water to meet the demands.&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:53.120
Scott Seus is a father of three and a third&nbsp;
generation farmer within the Klamath Basin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.640
a huge man-made irrigation system.
We don’t want to see bloom in the alfalfa like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.920
this. This is a crop under stress and it’s under&nbsp;
stress because we can’t get enough water to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:09.920
We are trying to take a very limited groundwater&nbsp;
resource and trying to spread it over a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:17.920
acres to try to keep our perennial crops alive.
Scott's 400-acre farm is at an elevation of 4000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:25.120
feet. Besides alfalfa, he is growing&nbsp;
onions, peppermint, garlic and horseradish.&nbsp;

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:32.560
I’m not willing to stand aside and roll over for&nbsp;
this. I’m willing to fight for it because it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:38.880
disrespectful to my dad and it’s disrespectful to&nbsp;
my grandfather and it’s honestly not right for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.800
not to give my children the opportunity to raise&nbsp;
their family here and to have their kids have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.960
opportunity to farm. That’s my responsibility&nbsp;
and that’s why I don’t walk away from it.&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:55.920
Ben Duval has a much smaller farm,&nbsp;
but shares the same problems.&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.240
Looking at something like climate change is very&nbsp;
concerning, because what does that mean for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:06.880
sustainability of what we are doing here into&nbsp;
the future. And at the same time I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:13.120
irrigation is a great way for us to be able to be&nbsp;
insulated somewhat from climate change, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.960
we are not dependent on rainfall in a particular&nbsp;
month. We can average it out at least over a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:24.880
or maybe over several years, if you have enough&nbsp;
storage capacity in reservoirs. It gives us an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:29.840
opportunity to be able to produce crops even&nbsp;
when we are seeing more extremes in climate.&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:39.120
Ben wants his daughters to continue the family&nbsp;
tradition. He lost a leg in a car accident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.680
but that hasn’t stopped him from&nbsp;
fighting for his family’s right to farm.&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.720
The fight for water has already mobilized&nbsp;
some farmers, who set up a full time protest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:00.000
next to the channels that regulate how&nbsp;
much water they are allowed to access.&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.000
SOT Dan Nielsen, protester
We are just here trying to educate people, trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:08.400
to get them to stand up and take their water. I&nbsp;
mean, it’s our water, I mean we do got to take it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.200
stand up and get it, or, or we gonna loose it. We&nbsp;
gonna have to forcefully take it, and then we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.280
to get the sheriff or someone who have to come&nbsp;
and help us and we gonna have to take it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.560
get it flowing or we not gonna have water here.
This is the very place where officials decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:28.320
how the water is divided. Do they divert it to&nbsp;
the farmers for their crops or do they keep it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:35.360
in the lakes and rivers on the native lands.
With too little water and the extreme heat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:41.280
fish species which are central to the diet and&nbsp;
culture of local native tribes are endangered.&nbsp;

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:51.920
All this biomass that we see here and by the fall&nbsp;
all this will have decomposed and resulted in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:59.440
the death of all the young of the year fish.
Alex Gonyaw is a biologist who is monitoring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:06.240
the quality of the water and the status of&nbsp;
the fish population for the Klamath tribes.&nbsp;

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:11.040
In the short term there’s not much we can&nbsp;
do about climate change, however we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:18.880
alter the water quality in a positive way,&nbsp;
that means fencing cattle off of streams not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:25.600
adding any additional phosphorus to the system and&nbsp;
allowing the aquaticus ecosystem to heal itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:32.800
Nature is amazing at doing that, but first we&nbsp;
need to remove the human pressure from the system.&nbsp;

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:39.440
The things that the native American tribes,&nbsp;
the Klamath tribes here value have been largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:47.520
completely disregarded during the settlement&nbsp;
process, and it continues to be almost disregarded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:56.480
in terms of striking a balance between the eco&nbsp;
system, the values of the tribes, agriculture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:01.840
and the treatment of the landscape in&nbsp;
general. It’s been extremely one sided.&nbsp;

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.880
Don Gentry is the chairman of the Klamath Tribes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:10.320
The Klamath have been living in&nbsp;
this area for thousands of years.&nbsp;

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:14.080
When you look out in the distance&nbsp;
and you see the cascades and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:19.840
how beautiful it looks and then you look into&nbsp;
the green water that looks like pea soup this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:25.360
time of year knowing that that creates conditions&nbsp;
that are lethal to our fish, it’s heartbreaking.&nbsp;

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:29.840
We are people of the lakes, marshals and&nbsp;
streams. Everything that the creator placed here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:37.520
in the rivers and marches and the lakes and on the&nbsp;
landscape was here and provide subsistence for us-&nbsp;

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:45.320
All those resources even in our legends and&nbsp;
stories remind us of how we are to be and what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:50.640
are not to how we are to live, that remind us that&nbsp;
the creator placed these things here for us for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:56.800
our subsistence but to never abuse that, you know,&nbsp;
we take only what we need, and use everything what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:05.600
we take, you know, we are are a part of things.
I am definitely concerned about violence and just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:13.360
continued racist based marginalization of who&nbsp;
we are and what those fish mean to us. We are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:19.280
portrayed as not being as important as other&nbsp;
folks to generate a lot of money to agriculture.&nbsp;

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:26.240
There hardly seem to be any possible&nbsp;
winners in this war over water.&nbsp;

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:30.000
But there will be many losers, if&nbsp;
the climate crisis isn't stopped.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:39.840
Charlotte Seus, whose father could&nbsp;
swim in the lake, might be one of them.

